Z||000002||Oreif||04-06-2001||06:34 PM||||AutoZone sells carbon metallic pads. They produce less dust and are less expensive than the Fiero Store brand. Also the Fiero Store brand is different in when cold they don't work as well. The autoZone brand is much better.||||reg||1
Z||000003||85Josh||04-06-2001||07:06 PM||||Hey, Orief, do you have a part # for those carbon metallics? the AutoZone near me only has standard Albany pads. They aren't jerking me around, they're pretty nice guys when they aren't busy.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||04-06-2001||07:32 PM||||There are a few formulas that claim less dust. check raybestos web site and such.<P>Brake dust is a tricky issue. This has been a puzzle ever since Front Disk became common in the 70's. <P>The pads must wear in order to work. Ideally what you want is dust that is less clingy, not a harder pad. (Hard pads can wear the rotor...) <P>It's hard because the small dust particles tend also to have allot of static electicity on them. the stuff wants to stick to the closest object with the oposite charge. (It's basically the same way powder coating guns work.)<P>I've dug around some on web sites like American Racing, a wheel maker. AR recomends frequent cleaning with soap and water. At last check they don't like any of the wheel cleaning product on the market.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Taijiguy||04-06-2001||08:07 PM||||If you got those carbon-mets from Autozone, I want the number. The A-Z down the road from me said they non gots. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||montage||04-07-2001||03:17 PM||||I would think that the carbon composite brake pads would create less brake dust because carbon is actually conductive therefore is would not attain a charge. This could cause less static cling to the wheels. This of course depends on whether static is the biggest factor in causing the dust particles to cling to the wheel. I would also speculate that coated wheels also contribute to this problem so wheels with a metallic finish may also decrease brake dust build up. I'd be intereseted in comments by members who have the carbon pads in regards to dust build up and how well they work.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Rodney||04-07-2001||03:42 PM||||What about these new ceramic pads. My 96 Cougar has this problem bad! ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Black88GT||04-07-2001||03:53 PM||||There is a product that you coat your wheels with that will prevent brake dust from getting all over your wheels.  I can't think of it right now but when I do I will post again.  I heard its the only one that works and you won't have to wash your wheels every week.  It bonds chemically.  I will think of it soon just give me time...<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD - MAFOA<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://88database.homestead.com/files/AF.gif">  <BR><I>"Once you go black, you never go back..."</I>||||reg||12
Z||000009||montage||04-08-2001||12:32 AM||||I would think that since carbon is conductive it would not charge like a typical organic brake pad material and therefore would not statically cling to wheels. In addition alloy wheels with a polished or chrome finish would also decrease the amount of static cling (conductive) compared to coated or painted wheels. I would be interested in any comments members that have carbon pads would have on the subject. I'm looking at carbon pads in the next brake job.||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||04-08-2001||12:56 AM||||ill wait to see some feedback on this, im gonna need new shoes all around after this summer. I DO KNOW - DONT use 'Klean wheels' or similar disks behind your wheels, I tried that route when I had thin 5 spoke Ferrari type. All my rotors were warped bad in a week. I had to have them all turned, been fine now for last 2 years. If anyone wants a set I do still have them for free if you pick them up. (they do make cool frisbees)||||reg||1
