Z||000014||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||04-11-2001||04:15 AM||||Ummmm . . . just because a particle is conductive does not mean it cannot acquire and maintain a charge.  Aerosolized particles are suspended in air and thus electrically isolated.  <P>When something generates dust from rubbing a process called triboelectric charging is likely to occur.  It's like rubbing rabbits fur on an amber rod (remember high school physics?). <P>The charge population (percentage of + and - particles present) of the ejected aerosol is depended on the materials rubbing together and the material present in aerosol. And, the humidity of the atmosphere but let's keep it simple. <P>Since the wheel is conductive and in contact with the brake rotor, it is likely to build up an opposite electrical charge to the brake pads. The idea is that electrons are being exchanged as the result of dissimilar materials rubbing. <P>Hence, the majority of brake pad dust particles will have an opposite charge to the wheel.  (not all, there are no absolutes in this reality) If the chares are sufficient, the particles attract to the wheel and deposit by electrostically-induced sedimentation.  Once deposited, their high ratio of surface area to mass means they will stick.  <P>Remember mass is related to volume (the cube of radius) and viscous drag (the force that keeps particles in aerosol while suspended) is dependant on surface area (the square of radius).  So, when a particle is small (&lt;1/1,000,000th of a meter) it will stay suspended in aerosol for several minutes.  On the other hand, a bowling ball falls at 132 MPH if given room to accelerate before hitting the ground.<P>I am sure none of you expected to be bombarded by aerosol science in a Fiero Forum, but I could not resist. Electrostatic theories about particles make photocopiers work and most PhDs really confused.  Electrostatics is an incredibly complex science.<P>But, after all the science, the brake dust is still going to stick to your wheels.  Even if you find a magic aluminum brake pad that has identical triboelectic properties to your wheels, half of the brake dust will still stick.  This is because the particles are still electrically isolated, so half of them will be charged - and the other half +.  The wheel will can acquire charge from the rubbing of the tire against the road.  After all, electrostatics is about high voltages and small currents.  <P>Want to keep brake dust off of your wheeels?  Get some steel wheels with nice solid hub caps.  Or, I think they used to sell brake dust shields for alloy wheels.<P>But is it worth it?  Those holes in your wheels help to cool your brakes and prevent fade.  The lesson here is that Safety must come before cosmetics!  <P>(Lucky climbs down from his soapbox hoping no one will realize he is a closet science geek)||||reg||4
Z||000015||Tigger||04-11-2001||08:49 AM||||Brake less often?<P>j/k, couldn't resist  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I waxed the snot out of my wheels before I put them on and going to do it again this spring.  Although I can see a slight buildup after a week or two it seems to help keep the dust from sticking.||||reg||1
Z||000016||montage||04-11-2001||03:00 PM||||Opps sorry about double post computer hick up.<P>This all assumes that electrostatic charge is the major problem with brake dust accumulation. Several other mechanisms could be at work like particle size of the dust if small enough could cause other forces to come into play (we are already way too deep so lets not go there).<P>First of all a conductive particle cannot maintain a charge unless it is electrically isolated and a charge is induced. The triboelectric property of the materials involved determines how much charge if any is introduced in the materials. Just because the rotor is conductive does not mean it will build up and opposite charge to the brake pads this is determined by the triboelectric properties of the materials involved and if the conductor is electrically isolated. I'm assuming that the wheel has a conducting path to the frame but am not sure if this is true. If this is not true than another solution would be to attach a wire from the control arm to the frame to provide a conduction path. <P>The bonding of conductive materials is metallic so you could imagine a sea of electrons that are very mobile (why it is conductive, mobile charge carriers). A insulator (non-conductive material) usually has a ionic bond in which you have; one element that gives up one or more electrons and is a cation (+charge) and one element that wants one or more electrons and is an anion (- charge). This sets up an electrostatic field that produces a very strong bond. If you rub two insulators together (carpet and shoes, balloon and hair, some conductors or metals are triboelectric provided electrically isolated) they will exchange charges form adhesion and become electrostatically charged. If you rub any material on a metal that is not electrically isolated there is no charge exchange. However, two dissimilar metals in contact will produce the Seabeck effect and produce a potential due to the difference in the Fermi energy level of the electronic structure this is how thermocouples (measure heat) work. A similar effect is the Peltier effect in which one can produce heat (and cooling) by applying a current to two dissimilar metals in contact.<P>If the wheel has a conductive path to the frame then it is at ground potential but if it is coated (unless chromed) this is usually a non-conductive material making the surface non-conductive. If the dust is charged and comes into contact with a ground potential then is will dissipate its charge and become charge neutral and therefore no static cling. If the wheel is coated then the charge cannot dissipate and you have static cling. Since the wheel is at ground potential and if it is not coated then it would dissipate the charge on the particle much like the wrist straps you are suppose to wear when touching computer chips (static electricity can exceed the voltage rating and destroy the chip). If the brake rotor is at ground then particles will not get electrostatically charged. However, brake pads are usually made of a variety of materials (mostly organic type materials and other insulators) and the contact of these particles with each other could cause electrostatic charge (in addition there could be a non-conductive film on the rotor surface produced from the brake pad). If the brake pad materials is conductive (or a majority) then this could improve the conductive path to the rotor and reduce electrostatic charge that is developed in the dust particles. <P><BR>I think that the most important factor is the surface conduction of the wheels with chrome plating the best conductor. Polished Al is better then coated wheels (paints and polymers) but aluminum oxide forms on the surface and could contribute to the static cling. In the end I speculate that brake pads with conductive materials and chromed wheels would provide the least amount of brake dust build up. It would be interesting to try and determine if the wheel is at ground if not then a wire from the control arm to the frame would also help reduce brake dust. Anyone notice if metallic brake pads produce less dust than standard pads? Of course in the end this may be so small of a change that it may not matter but is an interesting subject.<BR>||||reg||1
